[{:rule-id "rule-ai", :item {:feed-id "hn", :item-id "https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18861", :title "Hybrid Reasoning Agents: LLM agents that think in latent space, act through text", :link "https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18861", :published-at #inst "2026-10-18T20:48:14.000-00:00", :content "Comments"}, :excerpts [{:text "Hybrid Reasoning Agents: LLM agents that think in latent space, act through...", :matched-terms ["llm"], :source :title}]} {:rule-id "rule-ai", :item {:feed-id "hn", :item-id "https://jrdsgl.com/if-you-dont-run-your-own-llm-proxy-how-the-fuck-do-you-even-know-whats-going-on/", :title "If you don't run your own LLM proxy, how do you know whats going on?", :link "https://jrdsgl.com/if-you-dont-run-your-own-llm-proxy-how-the-fuck-do-you-even-know-whats-going-on/", :published-at #inst "2026-10-18T21:09:12.000-00:00", :content "Comments"}, :excerpts [{:text "If you don't run your own LLM proxy, how do you know whats going on?", :matched-terms ["llm"], :source :title}]} {:rule-id "rule-ai", :item {:feed-id "hn-frontpage", :item-id "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50145715", :title "Show HN: WhatsApp MCP running free on Cloudflare", :link "https://github.com/finereli/whatsapp-mcp", :published-at #inst "2026-10-18T21:36:10.000-00:00", :content "I wanted my humble Claude on mobile to access my WhatsApp messages but the only options I could find either required WhatsApp Business or an actual server to run Baileys on. I wanted to run it for free on ClaudeFlare and with my personal WhatsApp account, so I built this. Important feature - it connects to your Google account as well as WhatsApp to grab the names of all your WhatsApp contacts, otherwise you get only phone numbers for many of them and search doesn't work properly. This is a single user install because it's both gray area and very private - so point your agent at the repo and let it set things up. Comments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50145715 Points: 58 # Comments: 38"}, :excerpts [{:text "I wanted my humble Claude on mobile to access my WhatsApp messages but the...", :matched-terms ["claude"], :source :content} {:text "... run Baileys on. I wanted to run it for free on ClaudeFlare and with my personal WhatsApp account, so I...", :matched-terms ["claude"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "clojure", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/vimslime", :title "vim-slime", :link "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/vimslime", :published-at #inst "2026-10-19T03:51:33.000-00:00", :content "<!DOCTYPE html> <html> <head> <title>Tarn Barford</title> <meta charset=\"utf-8\"/> <link rel=\"icon\" type=\"image/x-icon\" href=\"/favicon.ico\"> <link href=\"/style.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link rel=\"alternate\" type=\"application/atom+xml\" title=\"Journals of Tarn Barford\" href=\"/atom\" /> <link href=\"/highlight.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link href=\"/highlight-console.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> </head> <body> <div id=\"container\"> <div id=\"header\"> <div id=\"header\"> <p>From the <a href=\"/journal\">Journals</a> of <a href=\"/\">Tarn Barford</a></p> <h1> vim-slime </h1> <p> Mar 26, 2012 </p> </div> </div> <div id=\"post_content\"> <html><body><p>Today I found the awesomeness that is <a href=\"https://github.com/jpalardy/vim-slime\">vim-slime</a>, it's been an exciting day for me. <a href=\"http://common-lisp.net/project/slime/\">Slime</a> is the \"The Superior Lisp Interaction Mode for Emacs\", I can almost hear the emacs crowd laughing.</p> <p>For those that use vim and haven't used Slime, vim-slime or <a href=\"https://github.com/vim-scripts/VimClojure\">something similar</a>, this is why it's awesome:</p> <p><strong>Text can be sent from any process to the stdin of a <a href=\"http://www.gnu.org/software/screen/\">gnu screen</a> or <a href=\"http://tmux.sourceforge.net/\">tmux</a> session. The process in this case is vim and the screen/tmux session is a terminal</strong>.</p> <p>Screen is a <a href=\"/journal/oh-screen-where-have-you-been\">really neat</a> terminal multiplexer (you can run multiple terminals in a terminal window). The multiplexed shell processes are children of the screen process, which itself is not a child of the terminal window process. This means a screen process and its child processes keep running if you close the terminal window. Later you can re-connect to it, this is what makes vim-slime possible.</p> <p>Here is an screen shot, on the left is me in gVim writing some awful Clojure <a href=\"#footnote-1\">[1]</a>. On the right is a screen buffer in which I started a Clojure REPL. When I want to try run some code I can send any vim text selection to the REPL in a keystroke (or two).</p> <p><img alt=\"vim slime screenshot\" src=\"screenshot.jpg\"/></p> <p>It doesn't have to be a Clojure REPL either, we can send anything to a screen shell. We could run git commands, find, grep, sed, etc. Like with the Clojure REPL we can even interact with any terminal programs that use STDIN.</p> <p>This concept can be taken even further, You can even connect to a tmux session over SSH and share a terminal or a <a href=\"http://remotepairprogramming.com/remote-pair-programming-with-tmux-and-vim-the\">terminal program like vim to do remote pairing</a>!</p> <p>Hopefully remote pairing is the topic of my next post as there are a couple geographically distant people I know who are keen to do some pair hacking. I stand to learn a lot!</p> <p><a name=\"footnote-1\">[1]</a> I learnt almost everything I know about Lisp from <a href=\"http://www.ccs.neu.edu/home/matthias/BTLS/\">The Little Schemer</a>. Great book.</p></body></html> </div> <div id=\"comments\"> </div> </div> <div id=\"footer\"> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>Questions, comments, suggestions? Email me, <a href=\"mailto:tarn@tarnbarford.net\">tarn@tarnbarford.net</a> (<a href=\"/pgp.txt\">public key</a>)</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> </div> </body> </html>"}, :excerpts [{:text "... or <a href=\"https://github.com/vim-scripts/VimClojure\">something similar</a>, this is why it's...", :matched-terms ["clojure"], :source :content} {:text "... on the left is me in gVim writing some awful Clojure <a href=\"#footnote-1\">[1]</a>. On the right is a screen buffer in which I started a Clojure REPL. When I want to try run some code I can send any vim text selection to the REPL in a keystroke (or two).</p> <p><img alt=\"vim...", :matched-terms ["clojure" "repl"], :source :content} {:text "... <p>It doesn't have to be a Clojure REPL either, we can send anything to a screen shell. We could run git commands, find, grep, sed, etc. Like with the Clojure REPL we can even interact with any terminal programs...", :matched-terms ["clojure" "repl" "command"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "rescript", :item {:feed-id "planet-clojure", :item-id "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/swipe", :title "Swipe Keyboard", :link "https://tarnbarford.net/journal/swipe", :published-at #inst "2026-10-19T03:51:33.000-00:00", :content "<!DOCTYPE html> <html> <head> <title>Tarn Barford</title> <meta charset=\"utf-8\"/> <link rel=\"icon\" type=\"image/x-icon\" href=\"/favicon.ico\"> <link href=\"/style.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link rel=\"alternate\" type=\"application/atom+xml\" title=\"Journals of Tarn Barford\" href=\"/atom\" /> <link href=\"/highlight.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <link href=\"/highlight-console.css\" media=\"screen\" rel=\"stylesheet\" type=\"text/css\" /> <style> #swipe-canvas { position: relative; width: 900px; height: 300px; } #swipe-results { font-size: 30px; padding-left: 50px; padding-left: 50px; } #swipe-results ul { margin: 0px; padding: 0px; } #swipe-results li { float: left; background-color: #DDDDDD; list-style-type: none; padding: 10px; margin: 5px; border-radius: 5px; } #swipe { position: relative; } #swipe-loading { position: absolute; height: 50px; width: 300px; top: 85px; left: 300px; background-color: darkgray; border-radius: 10px; text-align: center; padding-top: 20px; border: black; border-width: 5px; } </style> </head> <body> <div id=\"container\"> <div id=\"header\"> <div id=\"header\"> <p>From the <a href=\"/journal\">Journals</a> of <a href=\"/\">Tarn Barford</a></p> <h1> Swipe Keyboard </h1> <p> Apr 06, 2014 </p> </div> </div> <div id=\"post_content\"> <html><body><p>When I first tried a <a href=\"http://www.swype.com/\">Swype</a> keyboard I was impressed how effective it was. Even though I don't use the feature on my phone I was interested in how it could be built, so I <a href=\"https://github.com/tarnacious/swipe-keyboard\">implemented this otherwise useless swipe-able keyboard</a> below. It probably doesn't work on mobile devices, but works on modern browsers with mouse pointers (although I've only really tried Chrome and Firefox).</p> <div id=\"swipe\"> <canvas height=\"300px\" id=\"swipe-canvas\" width=\"900px\"></canvas> <div id=\"swipe-results\"></div> <div style=\"clear: both\"></div> <h2 id=\"swipe-loading\">Loading<noscript>Javascript is Required</noscript></h2> </div> <p>I initially tried to solve this using the technique Peter Norvig famously uses in his <a href=\"http://norvig.com/spell-correct.html]\">spell checker</a>. He takes a sequence of characters and generates a set of word candidates by adding, removing and swapping characters in the original sequence, the generated candidates are removed if they are not found a dictionary. This can work but to be effective too many combinations need to be generated.</p> <p>If the dictionary is indexed into a <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trie\">trie</a> the number of combinations generated can be reduced significantly by traversing the trie and only generating valid letter combinations. This is a pretty bare implementation of that, it requires: </p> <ul> <li>The first and last characters of the initial sequence are used </li> <li>Intermediate characters in the initial sequence can be repeated or discarded </li> <li>No characters are added or swapped</li> </ul> <p>Basically, if you swipe through all the characters in a word in order, then the word will be found if it is in the index regardless how many characters are swiped in between. It is surprisingly quick and effective.</p> <p>This implementation uses <a href=\"https://raw.github.com/first20hours/google-10000-english/master/google-10000-english.txt\">these 10000 words</a>, I intended to use digital books but never got around to it as these words demonstrate the concept well enough.</p> <p>This is the first thing I've written in <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">ClojureScript</a> or <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">Clojure</a>, so my code my vary from non-idiomatic to shamblolic. I initially used a <a href=\"http://clojuredocs.org/clojure_core/clojure.zip/zipper\">zipper</a> to build the trie with immutable data structures, but found the indexing took to long with my zipper implementation so I <a href=\"https://github.com/tarnacious/swipe-keyboard/commit/6edd7b26e78121fbe8586b3f0ef54ca8277d9e32\">switched to using native Javascript maps</a>.</p> <p>I found that <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/core.async\">core.async</a> library is really awesome, the <a href=\"http://docs.closure-library.googlecode.com/git/index.html\">Google closure library</a> and <a href=\"https://developers.google.com/closure/compiler/\">compiler</a> integration with <a href=\"http://leiningen.org/\">Leiningen</a> the <a href=\"https://github.com/emezeske/lein-cljsbuild\">cljsbuild plug-in</a> to be impressive. My main pains were the slow JVM start-up time, the advanced closure compiler build of the web worker script fails silently when run (but the main script works fine when compiled with the advanced compiler), and at times I felt some compile time type checking would be nice.</p> <p>I would like to extend this experiment to index the word occurrence counts and proceeding word counts in original text and rank the found words as most likely. Support casing, umlauts, special characters, spelling correction and compound words in the indexing and lookup. I think a live lookup while swiping would also be possible.</p> <p>Overall this was fun, turned out OK I think, and was a great learning experience.</p></body></html> </div> <div id=\"comments\"> </div> </div> <div id=\"footer\"> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>Questions, comments, suggestions? Email me, <a href=\"mailto:tarn@tarnbarford.net\">tarn@tarnbarford.net</a> (<a href=\"/pgp.txt\">public key</a>)</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> </div> <script src=\"swipe.js\" type=\"text/javascript\"></script> </body> </html>"}, :excerpts [{:text "... <h2 id=\"swipe-loading\">Loading<noscript>Javascript is Required</noscript></h2> </div> <p>I initially...", :matched-terms ["javascript"], :source :content} {:text "... in <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">ClojureScript</a> or <a href=\"https://github.com/clojure/clojurescript\">Clojure</a>, so my code my vary from...", :matched-terms ["rescript"], :source :content} {:text "... to using native Javascript maps</a>.</p> <p>I found that <a...", :matched-terms ["javascript"], :source :content}]} {:rule-id "rule-ai", :item {:feed-id "sean-goedecke", :item-id "https://seangoedecke.com/what-requirements-dont-you-have/", :title "What requirements don't you have?", :link "https://seangoedecke.com/what-requirements-dont-you-have/", :published-at #inst "2026-10-19T00:00:00.000-00:00", :content "When designing software, the requirements you don’t have are as important as the requirements you do. Identifying what you can compromise on — what can be slow, or stale, or inflexible — is usually the key to an elegant design. Elegant systems drop some requirements For instance, take preforking web servers like Unicorn. Because they lack the feature of shared mutable state between server instances, they can run each server in its own process, which provides isolation for free. Because Unicorn specifically ignores the problem of slow client handling1, it can queue requests directly on Unix sockets, drastically simplifying the implementation. Because S3 does not support atomic multi-key updates — you cannot update multiple blobs in a single transaction — it can store data at the blob level however it chooses. That makes it much faster, since reads and writes don’t have to cross storage partitions, and much easier to scale, since less coordination means fewer sources of truth. StatsD uses UDP by default, which means it doesn’t guarantee that every piece of data you send makes it into storage. In exchange for dropping that requirement, it incurs much less network overhead, which is a big advantage for a stats aggregation service that’s constantly shoveling data over the wire. The general design approach of fanning out data to an edge cache buys a lot of speed and scaling by dropping the requirement that data must always be up-to-date. SQLite buys operational simplicity — it can run out of a single file! — by dropping the requirement of concurrent writes. By accepting high tail latency2, Cursor can scale their distributed git backend more easily. Well-designed systems turn their weaknesses into strengths. Why dropping requirements is hard Anyone who’s spent time designing real-world software (or who’s studied distributed systems) knows that system design is a process of compromise. If you attempt to satisfy every single potential requirement — speed, concurrency, up-to-date data, a simple interface, supporting complex user behaviors — you will fail to design a working system at all. Figuring out what requirements you can relax is difficult because it combines both business and technical expertise. It is technically challenging to identify precisely the level of staleness that a particular design change will allow; it is likewise challenging from a business perspective to determine whether that level of staleness is acceptable to users. In most cases, an engineer who understands the technical side must try and explain it to a product manager, who can make a judgement on the business side. But this is a very difficult partnership to get right. Engineers and PMs have different incentives and little ability to check each other’s work, which makes it hard to build trust. Without trust, the product manager is liable to avoid giving any ground at all3, which leaves the design in an impossible state. This is an underappreciated reason why solo developers or startups can sometimes run rings around larger companies. Because the technical and business decisions are united in a single person, they can make design tradeoffs that would be impossible for a large company, enabling them to build systems that are far more elegant and powerful. Working with LLMs Knowing what requirements you don’t have becomes far more important when working with coding agents. Frontier models are very capable, but they have all gone through the purifying flames of RLVR: like high-achieving schoolchildren, they are obsessed with satisfying the Grader. On top of that, they have very little business context compared to an experienced human. That often manifests as them catching correctness or consistency “bugs” that are really just hypothetical unsatisfied requirements. For instance, it’s not uncommon for an LLM to casually introduce a mutex or other concurrency mechanism to ensure that two pieces of semi-related data are kept in sync, even if not told to do so. I’ve seen LLMs add weird checks in the middle of request handling to ensure that some already-checked piece of data hasn’t changed mid-flight, even if that wouldn’t successfully prevent the feared TOCTOU bug4. LLMs are obsessed with edge cases like “what if the current user loses their admin status in the middle of us handling one of their admin requests?”5, or “what if the cache in storage partition A is updated a nanosecond before the one in storage partition B, leading some users to see different data?“. Particularly if run in a loop or told to “make no mistakes”, they will pile on cases like these until they’ve transformed your four-line change into a thousand-line monster. This is why I’m confused by some people’s claims that code review is dead. About half the time I review a large AI-generated change, I notice some unnecessary requirement that makes the diff more complex. When I dig deeper, the LLM invariably introduced this requirement on its own. When working with a cache, I will often spell out in my prompt that I don’t mind the data sometimes being stale or briefly inconsistent between cache instances. When reviewing Golang code, the first thing I do is scan for added mutexes or layers of indirection that don’t look necessary to me. If you allow requirements like these to propagate unchecked through a codebase, they will petrify the system over time. The whole thing will be such a mess of checking and re-checking and holding locks and enforcing consistency that it cannot be scaled at all. Keeping systems elegant requires a ruthless focus: on accepting stale data or occasional tail latency, on avoiding all but critical instances of coordination, and in general on figuring out what requirements you don’t have to satisfy. The problem: if an HTTP request sent by someone with slow internet trickles in one packet per second, does that have to tie up a server process for the whole time? Unicorn’s answer is ‘yes, so put nginx or HAProxy in front of me’. ↩ When a read request hits a stale storage node, that node must update itself before responding. ↩ I’ve also seen this happen in reverse, where the product manager desperately tries to convince an engineer that some level of stale data is acceptable, while the engineer digs their heels in and insists on the “perfect” system. The consequences are similarly dire. ↩ Of course, simply doing a separate check shortens the TOCTOU window instead of removing it. ↩ In every single system I’ve worked on, it would be fine to simply carry out (or fail) the admin request: if somebody was allowed to perform some operation 10ms ago, it’s likely not a disaster to let them perform it now. ↩"}, :excerpts [{:text "... are far more elegant and powerful. Working with LLMs Knowing what requirements you don’t have becomes...", :matched-terms ["llm"], :source :content} {:text "... For instance, it’s not uncommon for an LLM to casually introduce a mutex or other...", :matched-terms ["llm"], :source :content} {:text "... in sync, even if not told to do so. I’ve seen LLMs add weird checks in the middle of request...", :matched-terms ["llm"], :source :content}]}]